VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-011
Reason for Visit: Acute psychiatric distress and severe physical exhaustion mimicking a nervous breakdown, necessitating urgent assessment to prevent immediate self-harm or further physiological decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and domestic partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a 48-hour history of uncontrollable weeping, complete inability to perform activities of daily living, and severe psychomotor retardation. Immediate, face-to-face out-of-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out an acute major depressive episode with intent of self-harm, as a delay until routine GP availability carried an unacceptable risk to patient safety.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have absolutely nothing left to give, but if I stop, everything falls apart. My body physically will not let me move anymore.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a progressive six-month history of profound emotional depletion from constantly educating others on basic equity concepts within their workplace, leading to a complete loss of personal boundaries. Over the past two weeks, they developed extreme cynicism regarding performative allyship and a deep resentment toward peers who possess the privilege to disengage. Over the last 48 hours, this chronic occupational stress culminated in a severe physical collapse; the patient became bedbound, developed an inability to initiate self-care, and experienced paradoxical feelings of immense guilt when attempting to step away. The partner notes a recently blunted affect during discussions of severe social injustice, previously a topic of intense passion, and a diminished capacity to provide emotional support at home.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound for 48 hours requiring assistance with hydration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of occupational burnout resulting in total long-term disability. Minimal immediate self-harm risk, but passive suicidal ideation (wishing to not wake up) is present due to sheer exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/90 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Patient appears profoundly fatigued, unkempt, making poor eye contact, and exhibiting psychomotor slowing.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild reactive tachycardia, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally, unlabored breathing. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Alert and oriented, but cognitive processing is significantly delayed. No focal motor or sensory deficits. Musculoskeletal: Generalized muscle weakness and heavy limbs without localized tenderness, consistent with severe fatigue. Educational depletion is evident; the patient is too exhausted to formulate complete sentences regarding their work, displaying a deeply blunted affect and physical signs of cynicism when their advocacy role is mentioned.
Environmental Observations: Workplace laptop and multiple unread books on systemic equity are piled next to the bed; the patient actively turns their head away from them.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescribed psychiatric medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Emotional Labor Burnout in DEI Advocacy - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Occupational problem) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute onset directly linked to an occupational trigger, and anhedonia being strictly localized to the advocacy role rather than a global loss of pleasure in all life domains.

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the rapid, 48-hour physical collapse, normal body temperature, and absence of delayed deep tendon reflexes.

Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (Myalgic Encephalomyelitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the sudden nature of the crash specifically following an intense period of occupational emotional labor, rather than an underlying chronic viral or immunological prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, functionally incapacitating exhaustion, reactive tachycardia (HR 98), and elevated blood pressure (142/90) resulting from overwhelming emotional labor. The presence of passive suicidal ideation and complete cessation of self-care mandated immediate out-of-hours evaluation to exclude an acute psychotic break or severe endogenous depression requiring emergency involuntary hospitalization. Delaying care until routine morning hours would have left the patient in an unsafe, unmonitored psychological state, firmly satisfying the necessity for urgent MBS after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict medical leave of absence starting immediately. Recommended usual GP perform psychometric confirmation using the Maslach Burnout Inventory and investigate for documented blunting of the cortisol awakening response.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild personal boundaries, implement a strict "auto-reply" on all work and advocacy communications for the next 7 days; visually seeing the automated response handle requests will positively reinforce the behavior of stepping back and lower the immediate anxiety of perceived failure. To manage the physical exhaustion, spend 15 minutes each morning sitting outside without any digital devices; pairing morning light exposure with physical rest will help reset the diurnal cortisol slope.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to occupational psychology for trauma-informed burnout management.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to secure a formal medical certificate and monitor the passive suicidal ideation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if passive thoughts of death evolve into an active plan to harm oneself, or if unable to take in adequate oral fluids leading to dizziness or fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic and require ongoing review.
Clinical Course: The profound physical exhaustion will slowly improve over 7 to 14 days with complete removal from the triggering environment. Total recovery requires long-term structural changes to occupational boundaries.

